cards.back:
```c
int arr[5];
int* ptr = arr;
```
Here, `arr` decays to a pointer to its first element (equivalent to `&arr[0]`), which is then assigned to `ptr`.

cards.front:
What is the output of `sizeof(arr)` vs `sizeof(ptr)` for the following code, and why?
```c
int arr[10];
int* ptr = arr;
printf("%zu\n", sizeof(arr));
printf("%zu\n", sizeof(ptr));
```
